      "text": "To convert the point \\((0, -3\\sqrt{3}, 3)\\) from rectangular coordinates to spherical coordinates \\((\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)\\), we need to use the following formulas:\n\n1. \\(\\rho = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2 + z^2}\\)\n2. \\(\\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)\\) (adjusted for the correct quadrant)\n3. \\(\\phi = \\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{z}{\\rho}\\right)\\)\n\nGiven the point \\((0, -3\\sqrt{3}, 3)\\):\n\n1. Calculate \\(\\rho\\):\n\\[\n\\rho = \\sqrt{0^2 + (-3\\sqrt{3})^2 + 3^2} = \\sqrt{0 + 27 + 9} = \\sqrt{36} = 6\n\\]\n\n2. Calculate \\(\\theta\\):\n\\[\n\\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{-3\\sqrt{3}}{0}\\right)\n\\]\nSince the point \\((0, -3\\sqrt{3})\\) is on the negative \\(y\\)-axis, \\(\\theta\\) must be in the \\(\\pi\\) quadrant. The \\(\\tan^{-1}\\) function gives us a value in the range \\(-\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\) to \\(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\), but we need to adjust for the correct quadrant. For the \\(y\\)-axis, \\(\\theta = \\frac{3\\pi}{2}\\). Thus, \\(\\theta = \\frac{3\\pi}{2}\\).\n\n3. Calculate \\(\\phi\\):\n\\[\n\\phi = \\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{3}{6}\\right) = \\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) = \\frac{\\pi}{3}\n\\]\n\nSo, the spherical coordinates are:\n\\[\n(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi) = \\left(6, \\frac{3\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)\n\\]\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\left(6, \\frac{3\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)}\n\\]",
