      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "In rectangular coordinates, the coordinates $(x, y, z)$ are related to spherical coordinates $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)$ by the formulas:\n\\[ \\rho = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2 + z^2}, \\]\n\\[ \\theta = \\arctan\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right), \\]\n\\[ \\phi = \\arccos\\left(\\frac{z}{\\rho}\\right). \\]\nWe start by calculating $\\rho$:\n\\[ \\rho = \\sqrt{0^2 + (-3\\sqrt{3})^2 + 3^2} = \\sqrt{0 + 27 + 9} = \\sqrt{36} = 6. \\]\nNext, we calculate $\\theta$:\n\\[ \\theta = \\arctan\\left(\\frac{-3\\sqrt{3}}{0}\\right). \\]\nSince $\\tan(\\theta)$ is undefined when the numerator is the same sign as the denominator, and here the numerator is negative and the denominator is zero, $\\theta$ must be $\\frac{3\\pi}{2}$. (Note that $\\arctan\\left(\\frac{-a}{0}\\right) = \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\text{ or } \\frac{3\\pi}{2}$ depending on the sign of the sign, but here the angle is in the fourth quadrant, so it's $\\frac{3\\pi}{2}$).\nFinally, we calculate $\\phi$:\n\\[ \\phi = \\arccos\\left(\\frac{3}{6}\\right) = \\arccos\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) = \\frac{\\pi}{3}. \\]\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates are:\n\\[ \\boxed{\\left(6, \\frac{3\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)}. \\]",
